INT. TIDAL STATION KARST-9, CONTROL DECK - NIGHT

Emergency red. Sea slams the hull in slow, enormous beats. Consoles flicker.

VESNA DRAGOVIC, 40s, flight engineer, drags a sparking panel off its mount. AMARA OKORO, 30s, station medic, hangs from a ceiling rail with a pressure kit.

                    VESNA
          Hull's breached on four. How long?

                    AMARA
          Ninety seconds, maybe. Maybe less.

She hits her harness release and drops. Vesna catches her by the arm, then lets go quickly, as if burned.

                    VESNA
          Don't touch the wet floor. It's live.

                    AMARA
          Then why are you standing in it?

                    VESNA
          Because she's the only one who can reach the valve.

Which she? Neither answers. They both look at the valve wheel, high on the wall, and then at each other. She waits for the other to move.

                    AMARA
          (to herself)
          Breathe. Count. Move.

                    VESNA
          Do it!

Amara climbs the rack. Vesna steadies the base, jaw tight. The lights die. Only the emergency strip shows, a thin red seam.

                    VESNA (CONT'D)
          (shouting at the dark)
          Anyone still on the station, brace! Brace now!

Silence. Water on steel.

                    AMARA (O.S.)
          Nobody's here, Vesna. It's just us.

A heavy clunk. The wheel turns. The sea's rhythm slows, then stops. Lights stutter back on. Amara hangs from the wheel, laughing without sound.

                    VESNA
          She did it. I can't believe she did it.

                    AMARA
          I'm right here.

                    VESNA
          I know.

The wall behind them splits along a seam, and seawater sheets down, blank and cold. They stop laughing.

                    AMARA
          That's six.

                    VESNA
          Then we swim.
